dent :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{16}{3})^{k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186212063} {:answer/id #uuid "0b1c0c1c-6fd2-568b-a7a3-c7e5ba17018d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{16}{9})^{k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186212064} {:answer/id #uuid "23265560-ae67-5cc4-8080-2f4e88a075ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{4}{9})^{k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186212065} {:answer/id #uuid "75c04c60-31d7-500a-9f90-d4cb0153822a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{4}{3})^{k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186212066}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e7e07dbb-627d-525b-afc0-91e1e35865ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186212061}] :question/id #uuid "943f9246-f8df-50ce-89b2-cec84c65dc37" :question/math-academy-id "q-301701" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions is equivalent to $3^{-2k}4^{k}$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213703 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bff08c09-4836-5d3a-867d-3d48603490d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4\\sqrt{3})^{k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186212068} {:answer/id #uuid "f628ab58-29f4-5c22-9315-a1c4c0e7ed57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{4}{3})^{k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186212069} {:answer/id #uuid "20e7d168-c663-565c-9231-e33fb93f3071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(12)^{k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186212070} {:answer/id #uuid "258e048b-bbf8-5363-a22a-0a0cd8ca90a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(36)^{k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186212071} {:answer/id #uuid "51aae56e-9c19-53f0-bf5d-87bed0b05a48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{4}{9})^{k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186212072}] :answer-field/id #uuid "659e87eb-c15f-50a6-9cdd-04c6822b97d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186212067}] :question/id #uuid "1ced379d-9f4d-5522-8639-86bd00d1a3fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-301705" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions is equivalent to $3^{2k}4^{k}$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216814 :question/id #uuid "c06e2555-e66c-52b1-8d7c-3a3891eaa129" :question/math-academy-id "e-19617" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions has a factor of $2x - 5m$, where $m$ is a **negative integer** constant?\n\n1. $4x^{2} + 2x + 18m$\n2. $4x^{2} + 2x + 10m$\n3. $4x^{2} + 2x + 20m$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that by the factor theorem, if $(sx-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$, then\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{r}{s}\n$$\n\nis a root of the polynomial.\nThus, since $2x -5m$ is a factor of our polynomial $p(x)$, we must have\n\n$$\np\\left(\\dfrac{5m}{2}\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options in turn.\n\n- Option I, $p(x) = 4x^{2}+2x+18m$, is not valid. Here, we have\n$p(\\frac{3c}{2})|= 4(\\frac{5m}{2})^{2} + 2(\\frac{5m}{2}) + 18m; = 25m^{2} + 5m + 18m; = 25m^{2} + 23m; = m(25m + 23)$.\nThis equals zero only at $m=0$ or $m=-\\dfrac{23}{25}$, neither of which is a negative integer. ${\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n*Option II,*\n\n$$\np(x)=4x^{2}+2x+10m\n$$\n\nis not valid. Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(\\frac{3c}{2}) &= 4(\\frac{5m}{2})^{2} + 2(\\frac{5m}{2}) + 10m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 5m + 10m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 15m \\\\\n&= 5m(5m + 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equals zero only at $m=0$ or\n\n$$\nm=-\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nneither of which is a negative integer. ${\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n*Option III,*\n\n$$\np(x)=4x^{2}+2x+20m\n$$\n\nis valid. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(\\frac{3c}{2}) &= 4(\\frac{5m}{2})^{2} + 2(\\frac{5m}{2}) + 20m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 5m + 20m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 25m \\\\\n&= 25m(m + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equals zero only at $m=0$ or $m=-1$, and $m=-1$ is a negative integer. ${\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216818 :question/id #uuid "8f7c0c8c-7498-5fa7-82b7-01f45e456e32" :question/math-academy-id "e-19621" :question/problem "The expression $4x - g$ is a factor of $16x^{2} + 12x - 7g$. Which of the following could be the value of $g$?\n\n1. $g=0$ or $g=4$\n2. $g=4$ only\n3. $g=-1$ or $g=0$\n4. $g=0$ only" :question/worked-solution "Recall that by the factor theorem, if $(sx-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$, then\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{r}{s}\n$$\n\nis a root of the polynomial.\n\nNow, since $4x - g$ is a factor of the polynomial\n\n$$\np(x)=16x^{2} + 12x - 7g\n$$\n\nwe must have\n\n$$\np\\left(\\dfrac{g}{4}\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nSo, substituting\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{g}{4}\n$$\n\ninto the expression for $p(x)$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(\\frac{g}{4}) &= 16(\\frac{g}{4})^{2} + 12(\\frac{g}{4}) - 7g \\\\\n&= g^{2} + 3g - 7g \\\\\n&= g^{2} - 4g \\\\\n&= g(g - 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the zero product rule, we conclude that $g(g - 4)=0$ if\n\n$$\ng=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad g-4=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $g=0$ or $g=4$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216834 :question/id #uuid "8bb87cc9-61fc-51fd-b057-b223ea87235e" :question/math-academy-id "e-19646" :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = - 5 {x}^{2} + 10 x - 1 \\\\ y = 4 x - k\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is a constant, intersect at exactly one point in the $xy$-plane. Find the point of intersection." :question/worked-solution "The line $y=4x - k$ intersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny=-5x^2 + 10x - 1\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n-5x^2 + 10x - 1 = 4x - k\n$$\n\nWe need to determine the value of $k$ such that this equation has one real solution.\n\nA quadratic equation has exactly one real root if the discriminant is zero:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = 0\n$$\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5x^{2} + 10x - 1 &= 4x - k \\\\\n- 5x^{2} + 10x - 1 - 4x + k &= 0 \\\\\n- 5x^{2} + 6x + (k - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = -5, \\qquad b = 6, \\qquad c = k-1\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= 6^{2} - 4(-5)(k - 1) \\\\\n&= 36 + 20(k - 1) \\\\\n&= 36 + 20k - 20 \\\\\n&= 16 + 20k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo have exactly one real solution, we need\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = 0{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n16 + 20k &= 0 \\\\\n20k &= -16 \\\\\nk &= -\\frac{16}{20} \\\\\nk &= -\\frac{4}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the equation of the line is\n\n$$\ny=4x-\\left(-\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)=4x+\\dfrac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nTo find the $x$-coordinate, we solve the original quadratic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5x^{2} + 6x + (-\\frac{4}{5} - 1) &= 0 \\\\\n- 5x^{2} + 6x - \\frac{9}{5} &= 0 \\\\\n- 25x^{2} + 30x - 9 &= 0 \\\\\n- (25x^{2} - 30x + 9) &= 0 \\\\\n- (5x - 3)^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n5x - 3 &= 0 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{3}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the $y$-coordinate by substituting\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\ninto one of the two equations and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 4(\\frac{3}{5}) + \\frac{4}{5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{12}{5} + \\frac{4}{5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{16}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is\n\n$$\n\\Big(\\dfrac{3}{5},\\dfrac{16}{5}\\Big)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216835 :question/id #uuid "837bc91a-4f8a-5aff-af01-562ef7792052" :question/math-academy-id "e-19647" :question/problem "The function is defined by $f(x) = -a^{x} + d$, where $a$ and $d$ are positive constants. The graph of $y = f(x)$ in the $xy$-plane passes through the point $\\left(0, \\dfrac{5}{2}\\right)$, and the product of $a$ and $d$ is $\\dfrac{21}{2}$. What is the value of $a$?" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given conditions in turn.\n\n- Since the graph passes through the point $\\left(0, \\dfrac{5}{2}\\right)$, we have that $f(0) = \\dfrac{5}{2}$. So, we have the following equation, which we solve for $d{:}$\n$f(0)|= \\frac{5}{2}; - a^{0} + d|= \\frac{5}{2}; - 1 + d|= \\frac{5}{2}; - 1 + d + 1|= \\frac{5}{2} + 1; d|= \\frac{7}{2}$\n- Since $ad=\\dfrac{21}{2}$, using the fact that $d = \\dfrac{7}{2}$, we obtain the following equation, which we solve for $a{:}$\n$ad|= \\frac{21}{2}; a(\\frac{7}{2})|= \\frac{21}{2}; \\frac{7a}{2}|= \\frac{21}{2}; 2 \\cdot \\frac{7a}{2}|= 2 \\cdot \\frac{21}{2}; 7a|= 21; \\frac{7a}{7}|= \\frac{21}{7}; a|= 3$\n\nTherefore, $a=3$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216866 :question/id #uuid "18e1efbe-1992-5cb9-89cf-255ebe801347" :question/math-academy-id "e-19695" :question/problem "| $x$ | $f(x)$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $-4$ | $-5$ |\n| $2$ | $7$ |\n\nSome values of the linear function $f$ are shown in the table above. In the $xy$-plane, the line $y=f(x)$ passes through the point where $x=\\dfrac12$. Find the $y$-coordinate of this point." :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is a linear function, we know it must be of the form\n\n$$\nf(x) = mx + c\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is the slope, and $c$ is the $y$-intercept.\n\nFirst, we find the slope using the two given points\n\n$$\n(x_1, y_1)=(-4, -5)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(x_2, y_2)=(2, 7){:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= (7 - (-5))/(2 - (-4)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{12}{6} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope is $2$.\nNext, we find the $y$-intercept using one point. For $x=2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7 &= 2(2) + c \\\\\n7 &= 4 + c \\\\\nc &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function is\n\n$$\nf(x) = 2x + 3\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nto find the corresponding value of $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= f(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\\n&= 2(\\frac{1}{2}) + 3 \\\\\n&= 1 + 3 \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216878 :question/id #uuid "a7d8425c-67f2-5afd-859f-9b69e1ea1bfc" :question/math-academy-id "e-19709" :question/problem "If $3\\leq u \\leq 15$ and $4\\leq v \\leq 27$, what's the smallest possible value of $\\dfrac{u}{v}$?" :question/worked-solution "To minimize the quotient of two positive numbers $u$ and $v$, we take the smallest possible value of $u$ and divide by the largest possible value of $v$.\n\nMore precisely, if $a, b, c$, and $d$ are positive numbers such that\n\n$$\na\\leq u\\leq b\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad c\\leq v\\leq d\n$$\n\nthen the minimum value of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{u}{v}\n$$\n\nequals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmin(\\frac{u}{v}) &= (min(u))/(max(v)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{a}{d}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $\\min(u) = 3$ and\n\n$$\n\\max(v) = 27\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n(min(u))/(max(v)) = \\frac{3}{27} = \\frac{1}{9}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216979 :question/id #uuid "b32d0028-8e5c-5f8a-b989-a7ceff5c1fc6" :question/math-academy-id "e-20896" :question/problem "Show that $I(x) = \\dfrac{1}{xy^3}$ is an integrating factor of the equation \n\n$$\n(x^2y-y^3) \\,\\textrm{d}x - x^{3} \\,\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A function $I(x,y)$ is an integrating factor for the differential equation\n\n$$\nP(x,y) \\,\\textrm{d} x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nif the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\nI(x,y)[P(x,y) \\,\\textrm{d} x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y] = 0\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to check if the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x{y}^{3}}(({x}^{2}y-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x-{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y) & =0 \\\\ (\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\, \\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\, \\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}.\n$$\n\nFor our transformed equation, we have  \n\n$$\nP(x,y) = \\dfrac{x}{y^2}-\\dfrac{1}{x}, \\qquad Q(x,y) =- \\dfrac{x^2}{y^3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{x})=-\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}})=-\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x},\n$$\n\nthe transformed equation $\\text{is}$ exact.\n\nTherefore, $I(x)=\\dfrac{1}{xy^3}$ $\\text{is}$ an integrating factor for the initial differential equation."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216991 :question/id #uuid "20c69b24-dd3a-5da9-a537-d296430fd050" :question/math-academy-id "e-21083" :question/problem "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny = xy' + (y')^2+1.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny = xy' + g(y'),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of $y'.$\n\nTo solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny = xy' + (y')^2 +1,\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p = y'.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny = xp + p^2+1.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+{p}^{2}+1)^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+2p{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+2p)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $x + 2p = 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nx = -2p.\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute this into $y = xp + p^2+1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =xp+{p}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =(-2p)p+{p}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =-2{p}^{2}+{p}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =-{p}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the singular solution is given parametrically by\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2p \\\\ y &=-{p}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $p,$ we first solve the first equation for $p{:}$\n\n$$\np = -\\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nNext, let's substitute this expression for $p$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-{p}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =-{(-\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny = 1- \\dfrac{x^2}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216995 :question/id #uuid "e9bc71fc-3923-5a8d-aa53-0f8af18a7bbd" :question/math-academy-id "e-21513" :question/problem "In a science experiment, Priya heated a metal rod and then left it to cool in a room that maintained a constant temperature of $65^\\circ \\mathrm{F}$. At $3{:}00$ p.m., the rod’s temperature was $200^\\circ \\mathrm{F}$. After $5$ minutes, its temperature had dropped to $170^\\circ \\mathrm{F}$, and after $10$ minutes, it was $146.5^\\circ \\mathrm{F}$. The rod continued to cool over time.\n\nWrite the function that best models the temperature $T(t)$ in degrees Fahrenheit of the rod $t$ minutes after it was removed from the heat." :question/worked-solution "The temperature $T(t)$ at time $t$ is modeled by the equation\n\n$$\nT(t) = a \\cdot b^t + T_r\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $T_r = 65^\\circ \\mathrm{F}$ is the room (ambient) temperature,\n- $a$ and $b$ are constants to be determined, and\n- $t$ is the time, in minutes.\n\nAt $t=0$, the temperature is $T(0) = 200$. Therefore, we can determine the value of $a{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(0) &= 200 \\\\\na \\cdot b^{0} + 65 &= 200 \\\\\na \\cdot 1 &= 135 \\\\\na &= 135\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our model becomes\n\n$$\nT(t) = 135 \\cdot b^t + 65\n$$\n\nAt $t=5$, the temperature is $T(5) = 170$. Hence, we can determine the value of $b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(5) &= 170 \\\\\n135 \\cdot b^{5} + 65 &= 170 \\\\\n135 \\cdot b^{5} &= 105 \\\\\nb^{5} &= \\frac{105}{135} \\\\\nb^{5} &= \\frac{7}{9} \\\\\nb &= (\\frac{7}{9})^{1/5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function best models the temperature $T(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(t) &= 135 \\cdot ((\\frac{7}{9})^{1/5})^{t} + 65 \\\\\n&= 135 \\cdot (\\frac{7}{9})^{t/5} + 65\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217004 :question/id #uuid "bac67ae0-dc3c-528c-9e07-7a81caa86db4" :question/math-academy-id "e-21869" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\ny''+\\tan(x)y'+e^x y=0, \\qquad -\\frac{\\pi}{2} < x < \\frac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nSuppose $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(0)=4.$ What is $W\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)?$" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of $y'$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\tan(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-\\tan(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ W & =C\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=4$ to find $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\ 4 & =C\\cdot 1 \\\\ C & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=4\\cos x.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217410 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b005b27-af27-5491-84db-35da2d1b34b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3c/3c20fe680b61e536da86232e665d83c3bd9df7db597e76b7042d70e0c69b7eae.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295426} {:answer/id #uuid "39c74a2c-9f8c-5ee1-a486-d725b2c87b7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/50/5084d83e4a7bc0888d215023bbebd44eb1a29e16b4125a53ed3028ac5fd0f870.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295596} {:answer/id #uuid "ade1b294-25cb-5bfa-96d7-0abf79225d14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2b/2b0d1e883a48c60af3cb619e1107fc96f6ca9ce39ff599f3f7cd1fc711094502.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296182} {:answer/id #uuid "bd07be21-3dab-57a6-95e3-0f70bbd0a591" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b7/b773cea713701e8c15e0997223700d8c96abd46587735d5c1dcb2506e9c7fd8b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296262} {:answer/id #uuid "c9a75b25-89dd-5a3a-9929-85780f38e400" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5a/5af0a46e81f523a04b0400af187b310df907bab09ba1ce9957382bf07b38a42a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9a75b25-89dd-5a3a-9929-85780f38e400" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5a/5af0a46e81f523a04b0400af187b310df907bab09ba1ce9957382bf07b38a42a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296324} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f8b3cd9-1148-532a-9f87-f14b6ea4d053" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296785}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c2fee048-4447-5420-8324-3d8e1246582d" :question/math-academy-id "q-29379" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of  $y=-{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{x-3}?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{10})}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/d6/d61528c7868baa92a1adbb2c4b4b23f5c975df80dbbabfeb449ad73acea97802.png)\n\n \n\n- Horizontally shift it by $3$ units to the right to give $y={(\\frac{1}{10})}^{x-3}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(3,1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/3a/3a4e3b5a90900d204c3265187d01343233bcaa76d811276464742fa447e4543a.png)\n\n- Then, reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{x-3}.$ The point $(3,1)$ moves to $(3,-1)$ and the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/09/0936975d549f0f94f4c4139b9543142308c2cc56d38d1bba2c94dc6c681b3d99.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217485 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7b712d2-16a1-5409-aacc-776275887937" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z = \\frac{a}{b} + 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186215142} {:answer/id #uuid "23a282fa-add7-5b5b-a827-0e2707b452be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z = (\\frac{2a}{3b})^{2} - 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186215143} {:answer/id #uuid "29c75b65-b457-5b9a-9770-a6cce8fb8f33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z = (\\frac{a}{b})^{2} - 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186215144} {:answer/id #uuid "3c817c98-6a64-567c-bb95-14953aafc82f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z = \\frac{a}{2b} - 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186215145} {:answer/id #uuid "5fabcb5f-b3b1-51ba-a207-47817c3621ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z = \\sqrt{\\frac{2a}{3b}} - 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186215146}] :answer-field/id #uuid "25a8ad5c-9501-5b71-881a-175961b61058" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186215141}] :question/id #uuid "3d9bd274-5092-5487-9f87-7081980070ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-310311" :question/problem "The equation below relates the distinct positive real numbers $a$, $b$, and $z$:\n$\\frac{18a}{9b} = 3\\sqrt{z + 4}$.\nWhich equation expresses $z$ in terms of $a$ and $b$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217504 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac39afa9-3581-5a4f-8467-5b1595be906a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 3 - (\\frac{b}{10a})^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186215235} {:answer/id #uuid "ff164519-fb86-5a10-86e3-cf503baefcef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 3 - \\frac{b}{10a}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186215236} {:answer/id #uuid "0a99402d-52d9-50f1-acd9-35a245c967ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = \\sqrt{\\frac{b}{10a}} - 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186215237} {:answer/id #uuid "343b1a02-7173-5ebb-b0ff-383f0620f583" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = (\\frac{b}{10a})^{2} - 9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186215238} {:answer/id #uuid "34da70bf-6eb9-55fb-a1e0-ec133edf3456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 3 - \\sqrt{\\frac{b}{10a}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186215239}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d1bb7a88-1ce2-57d8-8ad7-e53557d0bb2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186215234}] :question/id #uuid "78c0fbd2-2105-58bb-a759-018e5885deb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-310512" :question/problem "The equation below relates the distinct positive real numbers $a$, $b$, and $x$:\n$5\\sqrt{3 - x} = \\frac{b}{2a}$.\nWhich equation expresses $x$ in terms of $a$ and $b$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217626 :question/answer